RayCAD patch to OCCT 8.0.1 (LGPL-2.1 with the OCCT exception; this file is under the same terms).

What: a cancel reaches more stages of a boolean, which a boolean or a feature removal can spend seconds to minutes in.
  - Message_ProgressIndicator gets a per-thread "current range" (ThreadRange, SetThreadRange, ThreadUserBreak and the
    scope helper ThreadRangeScope), for searches deep in TKGeomAlgo whose interfaces take no progress range.
  - BOPAlgo_EdgeFace and BOPAlgo_FaceFace (the edge/face and face/face jobs of BOPAlgo_PaveFiller) set their job's
    range as the thread's range while they run.
  - IntCurveSurface_InterUtils (the curve/surface intersection under IntTools_BeanFaceIntersector) checks it between
    the passes of its start-point sort and between start points; IntWalk_PWalking (the surface/surface walk under
    IntTools_FaceFace) checks it in its marching loop and while extending a line through a tangent zone. On a break
    they stop: the sort and the search return, the walk returns not done.
  - BOPAlgo_Builder::PostTreat sets its range as the thread's range while it corrects tolerances; the per-edge and
    per-face corrections under it (BOPTools_AlgoTools::CorrectPointOnCurve and CorrectCurveOnSurface, and in
    CorrectWires between vertices) skip their work on a break, and PostTreat then ends with BOPAlgo_AlertUserBreak.
  - BOPAlgo_PaveFiller::Perform sets its range as the thread's range for the whole run (a job's own range replaces it
    while the job runs). Under it, RemoveMicroSectionEdges stops between section edges (MakeBlocks then ends with
    BOPAlgo_AlertUserBreak), the shrunk-range jobs of FillShrunkData are skipped (left not done, which the filler
    already handles as a warning before its next check), and Approx_ComputeLine (the approximation of an intersection
    line in IntTools_FaceFace::MakeCurve) stops between its segments, not all done.

Why: RayCAD runs direct edits on a kernel thread it abandons on cancel and requires the abandoned OCCT work to stop
within 10 s (task T-080). On the NIST test models (tests/corpus/directedit/set_nist.tsv) edits ran on long after a
cancel: resizing a hole of NIST CTC 02 (AP203 with PMI) 161 and 167 s, removing a blend run of it 80 s (inside the
PaveFiller of BOPAlgo_MakerVolume), removing a hole stack of NIST D2MI 827-9999-904 40 s on Linux. gdb stack samples
taken while the abandoned thread ran showed one edge/face job inside IntCurveSurface_HInter::Perform
(IntCurveSurface_InterUtils::SortStartPoints, a bubble sort, and ProcessSortedPoints) and one face/face job inside
IntWalk_PWalking::Perform / ExtendLineInCommonZone, each for minutes; patch 0011 made jobs stop only between such
calls. With those searches stopped, the D2MI hole stack still ran on for up to 10.4 s, depending on when the cancel
came (cancels at 2 to 32 s), in stages that take seconds each on its long section curves: PostTreat's CorrectWires
(which intersects the 2D curves at every vertex of every face of the result), RemoveMicroSectionEdges and FillShrunkData
(which measure the length and box of every section edge), and GeomInt_WLApprox (which approximates a walked line).
A partial result is never used: BOPAlgo_PaveFiller checks the break after its jobs, after RemoveMicroSectionEdges and
in the steps after FillShrunkData, PostTreat after its corrections, and the operation ends with
BOPAlgo_AlertUserBreak. A run that is not cancelled is unchanged (nothing but the break is read).

Upstream: not reported.

